Hitchhiking is legal in England and no law forbids asking a stranger for a lift; the motorway network is the exception. [https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/1982/1163/made The Motorways Traffic (England and Wales) Regulations 1982] treat pedestrians as "excluded traffic" (reg. 3(d)) and regulation 15 lets them use a motorway only when an accident, an emergency or a broken-down vehicle makes it necessary — which puts the carriageway, the hard shoulder and the slip roads off limits. [https://www.gov.uk/guidance/the-highway-code/motorways-253-to-273 Rule 253 of the Highway Code] states the ban plainly and rule 271 adds that nobody may stop on a carriageway, emergency area, hard shoulder, slip road, central reservation or verge except in an emergency, so a lift cannot lawfully be picked up on the motorway itself. Motorway service areas are private land reached off the motorway rather than part of it, so asking drivers there is lawful, though the operator may ask you to leave. On A-roads and everything below them, stand safely off the carriageway and thumb — there is no offence in it.
